{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Make a stale clone's push **refuse** instead of silently re-unioning ~1 GiB of stripped history.\nThis is the keystone that makes the reclaim safe, and it must be deployed everywhere before the\nwindow. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why a guard is needed at all, and why in two places\nAfter the strip, `.bridge_state` still only appears from 2026-06-11, so the first **1,029 commits are\nbyte-identical** and `merge-base HEAD origin/tickets` **SUCCEEDS** (measured). So `sync.py` does NOT\ntake its unrelated-histories branch — it reaches the ordinary **diverged** path (`:100-112`) and\nunions normally. Quiescence does not close this.\n\nAnd a guard in `sync.py` alone is **bypassed**: `push.py:203` and `:229` run an INDEPENDENT\n`git merge <remote>/<branch>` on non-fast-forward rejection and then re-push (<=3 attempts), never\nconsulting `sync.py`. That is exactly the path a stale review-bot write takes. **[v2 CORRECTED — the earlier chain was\nwrong; plan-review caught it]** Verified chain: `voter.py:735 emit_code_review_artifact(...)` ->\n(within it) `rebar.create_ticket` -> `event_append.py:561 push.push_tickets_branch(...)` -> push\nrejected non-FF -> `push.py` merge -> retry **succeeds**. There is **no `push_after_commit`\nintermediary**: `push_after_commit` (`push.py:268`) is called only from `delete.py:337,397`,\n`compact.py:644,762` and `transition_close.py:757` — never from the create path. And `voter.py:735`\nis `emit_code_review_artifact(`, not `create_ticket`.\n\n## Placement (precise — the wrong spot wedges every clone)\n- Inside **`sync._union_merge`** (`sync.py:114`) — the sole shared callee of both `:97` and `:111`.\n  **NOT** before the merge-base test at `:96`: that would also block the ff-adopt at `:101-104`,\n  which is exactly how a merely-behind clone converges, wedging it permanently.\n- Before **`push.py:203` and `:229`**.\n- **NOT** at `lock.acquire()` (where `check_store_compat` already fires, `lock.py:296`): a blanket\n  write block would also block the convergence operation, deadlocking the clone it rejects.\n\n## Where the epoch lives\nAdd an `epoch` field to the **existing** `.store-compat.json` rather than a parallel marker.\nVerified safe: `compat.py`'s `_analyze` validates only `format_version` and `required_capabilities`\nvia `.get()`, so **unknown keys are ignored** — an older binary neither crashes nor fails closed.\nTwo precisions: `compat.write_compat_record` (`:184`) hardcodes the two keys, so a store it stamps is\nborn epoch-absent (decide that deliberately); and `_record_path` reads only the **local worktree**, so\nthe guard needs its own `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json` reader for the other side.\n\n## Expand/contract — the FULL state matrix (plan-review E2/T1 remediation)\nThe earlier wording said only \"`absent == absent` => compatible, `absent vs present` => abort\",\nwhich left two distinct absences conflated. Specify all of it. Two independent axes per side:\nthe **record** (`.store-compat.json`) may be absent or present, and within a present record the\n**`epoch` field** may be absent or present.\n\n| local | remote | behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| record absent | record absent | **compatible** (pre-feature store both sides; union proceeds) |\n| record absent | record present, epoch absent | **compatible** |\n| record present, epoch absent | record present, epoch absent | **compatible** (today's state) |\n| epoch present | epoch present, equal | **compatible** |\n| epoch present | epoch present, different | **ABORT** |\n| epoch absent | epoch present | **ABORT** (local predates the rewrite) |\n| epoch present | epoch absent | **ABORT** (remote predates local; refuse rather than guess) |\n| either side | record present but **corrupt/unparseable** | **ABORT** (never silently treat as absent — mirrors `compat.py`'s existing corrupt-record rule) |\n| remote ref unreadable (`git show` non-zero) | — | **ABORT**, distinguished in the message from \"file absent\" |\n\nNote the last two rows: `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json` exits non-zero **both** when the\nfile does not exist at that ref and when the ref itself cannot be read. These must be\ndistinguished in the log message, because the remedies differ (re-clone vs fix the remote).\n\n**Epoch value format (plan-review E2 remediation — previously unspecified).** `epoch` is a JSON\n**string**, opaque to comparison: compared for byte equality only, never ordered or parsed. Value =\nthe ISO-8601 UTC instant of the rewrite plus a short random suffix\n(e.g. `\"2026-08-14T09-31-07Z-4f2a\"`), unique per rewrite and human-diagnosable in a log line. A\nnon-string `epoch` (number, object, null) is treated as **corrupt** -> ABORT, matching `compat.py`'s\nexisting type-strictness for `format_version`. Deliberately NOT a monotonic counter: ordering invites\n\"newer is probably fine\" reasoning, and the only safe response to any mismatch is\nrefuse-and-re-clone.\n\nThe epoch **value** appears only in the reclaim's re-add commit. The epoch\n**value** appears only in the reclaim's re-add commit. That way pre-window clones (both absent) merge\nnormally even holding unpushed work, while post-rewrite stale clones abort.\n\n**The guard only binds binaries at or after this change** — older ones ignore the field. So the\nreclaim window instead requires direct deployment evidence: an observed `reconcile-bridge.yml` run on a\ndescendant SHA, plus deployed review-bot and explicitly refreshed host-runtime epoch-refusal probes; A6-0b deployment receipt and persistent-clone convergence stay in the protocol. These per-writer observations under quiescence replace elapsed time.\n\n## Control flow at each guard site (plan-review G6 remediation)\n`logger.warning` describes the *message*, not the *control flow* — and at the `push.py` sites both\nmerges sit inside the `for attempt` retry loop, so warning-and-continuing would simply retry the\npush. Specify both sites explicitly:\n\n- **`sync._union_merge`**: log and **return without merging**. `reconverge` is best-effort by\n  contract (its sole caller `reads.ensure_fresh` swallows everything), so a no-op return is the\n  correct degradation — the clone simply stays un-converged instead of unioning.\n- **`push.py` (before the merges at the non-FF retry)**: log and **abort the retry loop\n  immediately** — specifically **`return` out of `push_tickets_branch`, not `break`** (plan-review G6\n  remediation): `break` would fall through to whatever follows the loop and could still report a\n  completed push, whereas `return` exits with the push explicitly not-completed. Do not merge, do not\n  re-push, do not consume further attempts. The push must\n  surface as *not* completed so the existing `PUSH_PENDING` path (which `fsck` reports) is the\n  operator-visible signal. Returning \"success\" would silently drop the local commits from the\n  operator's view, and continuing the loop would burn attempts on a merge that must never happen.\n\n## Message channel\n`logger.warning`, **not** an exception: every caller of `sync.reconverge` is `reads.ensure_fresh`\n(`reads.py:255-262`), which wraps it in a bare `except Exception: pass` inside a second bare\n`except Exception: return`, so a raise is swallowed with **no output**. Visibility comes from the\nentrypoint handlers (`_cli/__init__.py:408`, `mcp_server.py:688`,\n`rebar_reconciler/__main__.py:438`), not Python's `lastResort` (`__init__.py:131` attaches a\n`NullHandler`, so lastResort never fires). Known gap: `sync.push=async` spawns the push with\n`stdout/stderr=DEVNULL` (`push.py:113-145`), so those clones get no message and rely on `fsck`'s\n`PUSH_PENDING`.\n\n## Out of scope, declared\n`fsck_recover.py` cherry-picks dangling ticket commits with no epoch awareness — it creates new\ncommits rather than unioning a DAG, so blast radius is bounded. Also **not** covered: the CI bash\nunions at `reconcile-bridge.yml:321` and `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml:439`, which are protected by\nthe window protocol (workflows disabled + no in-flight run asserted), not by this guard.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A clone on a **stale epoch** attempting to push **aborts and performs no merge** at **both**\n      guarded sites — asserted separately for `sync._union_merge` and for `push.py`'s non-FF retry.\n      Tier: **integration** in two real temporary clones with divergent epochs. This is the RED case.\n- [ ] Assert on the emitted **log record** (`caplog`), plus one **subprocess** run with\n      `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=always` proving the message reaches a real operator's stderr.\n      `sync.push=async` is excluded by design.\n- [ ] At the `push.py` site the retry loop **terminates immediately** on epoch mismatch: exactly\n      **zero** merge invocations and **zero** further push attempts, and the push reports\n      not-completed so `fsck` surfaces `PUSH_PENDING`. Tier: **unit** with a counting shim on the git\n      calls — the guard is a pure predicate plus a control-flow decision, so no real remote is needed;\n      the earlier \"integration\" label contradicted its own unit proving command (plan-review COH).\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_epoch_guard_matrix.py -k push_retry -v`.\n- [ ] At the `sync._union_merge` site the function **returns without merging** and the clone is left\n      un-converged (not corrupted). Tier: **integration**.\n- [ ] A merely-**behind** clone (matching epochs) still converges via ff-adopt, and a\n      **strictly-ahead** clone still returns early — neither is blocked. Tier: **integration**. This\n      is the negative control that catches the wedge-everything misplacement.\n- [ ] **Every row of the state matrix** above is asserted, including the two distinct absences\n      (record-absent vs record-present-epoch-absent) and the corrupt-record row. Tier: **unit**,\n      table-driven over the matrix — one case per row, so a missing row fails the suite.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_epoch_guard_matrix.py -v`.\n- [ ] `git show` failing because the **ref** is unreadable produces a different message than the file\n      being **absent** at a readable ref. Tier: **unit** with two fixtures.\n      Proving command: `pytest tests/unit/test_epoch_guard_matrix.py -k remote_read -v`.\n- [ ] An older binary reading a record that carries `epoch` does not error and does not fail closed.\n      Tier: **unit** against `compat._analyze`.\n- [ ] The remote side is read via `git show <remote_ref>:.store-compat.json`, not the local worktree.\n      Tier: **unit** with a fixture where local and remote records differ.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_store/compat.py`, `src/rebar/_store/sync.py`, `src/rebar/_store/push.py`, tests\n\nScope: M. No dependencies; must land and roll out before A6-1."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785785405337415001,"uuid":"53cd4cbe-4835-4387-ad95-e10dc55237ec"}